WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.018
Idea. Your body is built in levels. The smallest level is the cell. A specialised cell has features that suit it to one job. For example, a sperm cell has a tail to swim.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:20.417
Similar cells group together, and work together to do one job. This group is a tissue. Muscle tissue is one example.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:32.579
Then several different tissues work together to do a job. This is an organ. The heart is an organ. Its wall is mostly cardiac muscle tissue, but it has other tissues too.

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:43.856
Method. For a definition, write two linked parts: what it is made of, and that it does one job. A tissue is similar cells, working together for one function.

00:00:44.450 --> 00:01:07.803
An epithelium is a layer of cells that lines a surface. Squamous epithelium lines the cheek. Its cells are flat and uneven, with no cilia, and the nucleus in the middle. Ciliated epithelium lines the trachea, the tube that carries air to the lungs. Its cells are tall, with cilia on top. Cilia are tiny hairs. The nucleus is near the base.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:26.587
There are three kinds of muscle tissue. Look for three features: stripes, branches, and the number of nuclei. Skeletal muscle moves your bones. It is striped, not branched, with many nuclei. Cardiac muscle is in the heart wall. It is striped and branched, with one nucleus in each cell.

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:39.415
Smooth muscle has no stripes. Its cells are tapered, thin at each end, with one nucleus. It works without you thinking. It is in the gut wall, blood vessels, the uterus and the iris.

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:53.048
Worked example. Here is a real exam question. It shows a human body cell. Part three asks: explain how body cells are organised to form organs. It is worth two marks.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:02:01.322
Two marks means two steps. Step one: cells of the same type group together and work together. This group is a tissue.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:09.165
Step two: several different tissues work together. They form an organ, such as the heart.

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:16.693
Here is the full answer. This answer is written by olevels.com. It is not the official mark scheme.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:32.603
Common mistake. A common mistake is to write that a tissue is just a group of cells. That misses two ideas: the cells are similar, and they do one job together. Another mistake is to jump straight from cells to organs.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:44.143
Another mistake: naming a muscle or a bone as a type of tissue. Those are organs. Give the full tissue name, such as cardiac muscle or squamous epithelium.

00:02:45.250 --> 00:03:03.265
Your turn. The stomach wall has a lining of epithelium, a layer of connective tissue, and layers of smooth muscle. First, is the stomach a cell, a tissue or an organ? Explain. Second, describe what smooth muscle cells look like. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:15.907
Answer. Part a. The stomach is an organ. It is made of several different tissues: epithelium, connective tissue and smooth muscle. They work together to digest food.

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:23.763
Part b. Smooth muscle cells have no stripes. Each cell is tapered, thin at both ends, with one nucleus.

00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:38.486
So, every time: definitions have two parts, what it is made of and its one job. Cells form tissues, and tissues form organs. Name tissues in full. For muscle, check stripes, branches and nuclei.
